write nginx reverse proxy configuration that forwards port 8000 to 8011 and 8014<|end_of_text|>
<|assistant|>
To configure Nginx as a reverse proxy server that forwards incoming requests on port 8000 to ports 8011 and 8014, you need to create a new Nginx configuration file. Here's an example of how to set up this configuration:

1. First, create a new file called `proxy.conf` (or use an existing configuration file if you have one). The contents should look like this:
```nginx
upstream backend {
    server 127.0.0.1:8011;
    server 127.0.0.1:8014;
}

server {
    listen 8000;
    server_name localhost;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://backend;
        proxy_redir off;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        proxy_buffering off;
    }
}
```
1. Ensure you have the `http_proxy_module` loaded in your Nginx configuration. You can check this by running the following command:
```bash
sudo nginx -T -V
```
Look for `--with-http_proxy_module` in the output to see if it's enabled. If not, you'll need to recompile Nginx with this module or install a version that includes it.
2. Now, include the new `proxy.conf` file in your main Nginx configuration file, typically located at `/etc/nginx/nginx.conf`. Add the following line to the `http` section:
```nginx
include /etc/nginx/conf.d/*.conf;
```
This assumes that you've saved the `proxy.conf` file in the `/etc/nginx/conf.d` directory. If you have it in a different location, adjust the path accordingly.
1. Save the changes, then restart Nginx for the new configuration to take effect:
```bash
sudo systemctl restart nginx
```

Now, when Nginx receives incoming connections on port 8000, it will forward those requests to ports 8011 and 8014, distributing the incoming connections to both backend servers. The `proxy_buffering off;` directive ensures that Nginx does not buffer responses, allowing for faster response times.

Remember to replace `localhost` in the `server_name` directive with the hostname or IP address of your server if you're using a public domain name or IP for your server.<|end_of_text|>

